<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="other"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Indian J Dermatol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Indian J Dermatol</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="publisher-id">IJD</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Indian Journal of Dermatology</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">0019-5154</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1998-3611</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Medknow Publications &#x00026; Media Pvt Ltd</publisher-name><publisher-loc>India</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">23919045</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">3726922</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">IJD-58-330e</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.4103/0019-5154.114005</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>E-Quiz</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>A 54-Year-Old Female with Skin Lesions and History of Renal Cell Carcinoma</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Valizadeh</surname><given-names>Nasim</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="aff1"/><xref ref-type="corresp" rid="cor1"/></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="aff1"><italic>From the Department of Hematology/Medical Oncology, Urmia University of Medical Sciences, Urmia, Iran</italic></aff><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><italic><bold>Address for correspondence:</bold> Dr. Nasim Valizadeh, Department of Hematology/Medical Oncology, Urmia University of Medical Sciences, Urmia, Iran. E-mail: <email xlink:href="nsedaha0@gmail.com">nsedaha0@gmail.com</email></italic></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jul-Aug</season><year>2013</year></pub-date><volume>58</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage seq="e">330</fpage><lpage>330</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><month>6</month><year>2011</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><month>7</month><year>2011</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright: &#x000a9; Indian Journal of Dermatology</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2013</copyright-year><license license-type="open-access" xlink:href="http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0"><license-p>This is an open-access article distributed under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution-Noncommercial-Share Alike 3.0 Unported, which permits unrestricted use, distribution, and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited.</license-p></license></permissions></article-meta></front><body><p>A 54-year-old woman was admitted with left hip joint pain and limitation of motion. She had history of left-sided radical nephrectomy for renal cell carcinoma (RCC), chromophilic type 5.5 years ago. Her father and mother were dead because of malignancies a long ago (she did not know which type). One of her children was dead with leukemia, and she had 11 healthy children. Physical examination showed skin lesions (she had them since childhood) on her back of neck [<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Figure 1</xref>] and both shoulders [Figures <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">2</xref> and <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">3</xref>]. She had limitation of motion in left hip joint movements. Fasting blood sugar, complete blood count diff, blood urea nitrogen, creatinine, Na, K, aspartate transaminase, alanine transaminase, alkaline phosphatase, and albumin were reported normally. Electrocardiogram and chest X-ray were normal.</p><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Fibrofolliculomas (multiple pale dome-shaped papules) on the back of her neck</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJD-58-330e-g001"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Acrochordon: A large pedunculated fibroma on the right shouder</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJD-58-330e-g002"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Acrochordons (skin tags) on the left shoulder</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJD-58-330e-g003"/></fig><p>Further evaluation revealed RCC with metastasis to lungs [<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F4">Figure 4</xref>] in chest computed tomography scan and a large left hemipelvic mass with eroding of innominate bone, ischiopubic ramous, and partial impression of left femoral head in pelvic magnetic resonance imaging [<xref ref-type="fig" rid="F5">Figure 5</xref>] suggestive of metastasis.</p><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Bilateral multiple nodular metastasis in both lung fields is seen in chest computed tomography scan</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJD-58-330e-g004"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>A large left hemipelvic mass with eroding of innominate bone, ischiopubic ramous, and partial impression of left femoral head in pelvic magnetic resonance imaging suggestive of metastasis</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="IJD-58-330e-g005"/></fig><sec><title>Questions</title><p><list list-type="order"><list-item><p>What do you see in <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">Figure 1</xref>?</p></list-item><list-item><p>What do you see in Figures <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F2">2</xref> and <xref ref-type="fig" rid="F3">3</xref>?</p></list-item><list-item><p>Which syndrome do you consider for this patient?</p></list-item></list></p></sec><sec><title>Answers</title><p><list list-type="order"><list-item><p>Fibrofolliculomas (multiple pale dome-shaped papules) on the back of her neck</p></list-item><list-item><p>Acrochordons (skin fibromas)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Birt&#x02013;Hogg&#x02013;Dub&#x000e9; syndrome.</p></list-item></list></p><p>Birt&#x02013;Hogg&#x02013;Dub&#x000e9; syndrome is an autosomal dominant disease. In 1977, three Canadian physicians reported members of a family with multiple skin trichodiscomas, acrochordons (skin fibromas), and fibrofolliculomas.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref1">1</xref>] Affected gene is mapped on the 17 P chromosome.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref2">2</xref>] In 2002, Nickerson <italic>et al</italic>. discovered folliculin gene at this locus.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref3">3</xref>] Patients may suffer from pulmonary cysts, spontaneous pneumothorax, and renal tumors.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref4">4</xref>]</p><p>Multiple or bilateral RCCs most frequently of chromophobe and hybrid oncocytic histology have association with this syndrome, but clear cell and papillary RCC are less frequently found in this syndrome.[<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref5">5</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref6">6</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="ref7">7</xref>]</p></sec></body><back><fn-group><fn fn-type="supported-by"><p><bold>Source of Support:</bold> Nil</p></fn><fn fn-type="conflict"><p><bold>Conflict of Interest:</bold> Nil</p></fn></fn-group><ref-list><ref id="ref1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Birt</surname><given-names>AR</given-names></name><name><surname>Hogg</surname><given-names>GR</given-names></name><name><surname>Dub&#x000e9;</surname><given-names>WJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Hereditary multiple fibrofolliculomas with trichodiscomas and acrochordons</article-title><source>Arch Dermatol</source><year>1977</year><volume>113</volume><fpage>1674</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">596896</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref2"><label>2</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Khoo</surname><given-names>SK</given-names></name><name><surname>Bradley</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Wong</surname><given-names>FK</given-names></name><name><surname>Hedblad</surname><given-names>MA</given-names></name><name><surname>Nordenskj&#x000f6;ld</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Teh</surname><given-names>BT</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome: Mapping of a novel hereditary neoplasia gene to chromosome 17p12-q11.2</article-title><source>Oncogene</source><year>2001</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>5239</fpage><lpage>42</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11526515</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref3"><label>3</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nickerson</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><name><surname>Warren</surname><given-names>MB</given-names></name><name><surname>Toro</surname><given-names>JR</given-names></name><name><surname>Matrosova</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Glenn</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Turner</surname><given-names>ML</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Mutations in a novel gene lead to kidney tumors, lung wall defects, and benign tumors of the hair follicle in patients with the Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome</article-title><source>Cancer Cell</source><year>2002</year><volume>2</volume><fpage>157</fpage><lpage>64</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12204536</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref4"><label>4</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zbar</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Alvord</surname><given-names>WG</given-names></name><name><surname>Glenn</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Turner</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Pavlovich</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name><name><surname>Schmidt</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Risk of renal and colonic neoplasms and spontaneous pneumothorax in the Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome</article-title><source>Cancer Epidemiol Biomarkers Prev</source><year>2002</year><volume>11</volume><fpage>393</fpage><lpage>400</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11927500</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref5"><label>5</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Adley</surname><given-names>BP</given-names></name><name><surname>Smith</surname><given-names>ND</given-names></name><name><surname>Nayar</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>XJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome: Clinicopathologic findings and genetic alterations</article-title><source>Arch Pathol Lab Med</source><year>2006</year><volume>130</volume><fpage>1865</fpage><lpage>70</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17149965</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref6"><label>6</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Roth</surname><given-names>JS</given-names></name><name><surname>Rabinowitz</surname><given-names>AD</given-names></name><name><surname>Benson</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Grossman</surname><given-names>ME</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Bilateral renal cell carcinoma in the Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome</article-title><source>J Am Acad Dermatol</source><year>1993</year><volume>29</volume><fpage>1055</fpage><lpage>6</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8245249</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="ref7"><label>7</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Pavlovich</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name><name><surname>Walther</surname><given-names>MM</given-names></name><name><surname>Eyler</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name><name><surname>Hewitt</surname><given-names>SM</given-names></name><name><surname>Zbar</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Linehan</surname><given-names>WM</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Renal tumors in the Birt-Hogg-Dub&#x000e9; syndrome</article-title><source>Am J Surg Pathol</source><year>2002</year><volume>26</volume><fpage>1542</fpage><lpage>52</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12459621</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>